<article class="env-definition" id="00WM">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00WM">Definition <span data-tag="00WM">7.11.1</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a site, and let $\varphi : \mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{G}$ be a map of sheaves of sets. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say that $\varphi $ is <em>injective</em> if for every object $U$ of $\mathcal{C}$ the map $\varphi : \mathcal{F}(U) \to \mathcal{G}(U)$ is injective. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $\varphi $ is <em>surjective</em> if for every object $U$ of $\mathcal{C}$ and every section $s\in \mathcal{G}(U)$ there exists a covering $\{ U_ i \to U\} $ such that for all $i$ the restriction $s|_{U_ i}$ is in the image of $\varphi : \mathcal{F}(U_ i) \to \mathcal{G}(U_ i)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>